{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n1le01ob08re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n1le01ob08re2aud01", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob08re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Teacher:</strong> Today, we are talking about some programming concepts. Let's start with control statements. Why are they important? Well, you can't execute the sections of code in a program without using control statements. They are in charge of controlling the execution of those sections of code you want. Before using them, it is important to understand the types of control statements. Can you tell me something about them?\n</br><strong>Kate: </strong>There are three: sequential, decision, and iterative. Sequential, which is the default control structure, is the way in which you execute the statements one after the other in a certain order. Decision, or conditional - referred to as if, then and else - depends on the performance of a logical test that leads the statement to handle certain programming situations. With decision statements, you can't get a result without alternative execution of instructions. Finally, the iterative is the one in charge of controlling the number of times a code is executed. </br>", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob08re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Teacher: </strong>Great, Kate! I imagine you can also tell me how to structure conditional statements in different programming languages...  </br>\n<strong>Kate: </strong>Well, I know you can't do it without Boolean expressions\u2026 </br>\n<strong>Roger: </strong>If I'm not mistaken, all programming languages use specific statements. The condition particle is a Boolean expression that will evaluate if the certain computation is true or false. You can't have only one possibility! There are many ways to structure it according to the program's configuration. </br>", "time": "69000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob08re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Kate: </strong> Teacher, I want to know more about iterative constructs or loops. I know they control and iterate the number of repetitions a code is executed.  </br><strong>Teacher: </strong> Correct. They are divided in FOR loops and WHILE/DO loops. When you write FOR loops, you need three factors: the initialization, the condition, and the increment.  WHILE/DO loops only repeat a section of code if the condition is true; if it is not, it ends.", "time": "103000"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can list the types of control statements. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can say how to structure conditional statements in different programming languages.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can discuss basic information about iterative loops.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Why do you think each programming language uses different codes to achieve certain task?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Why do you think control statements are important in programming?", "type": "Free Header Item"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "Control Statements in Programming Languages", "type": "free_type_text_area"}]}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": 